WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.150
ED GLAESER: Skyscrapers may define modern urban skylines,

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:05.430
but cities existed for thousands of years

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:09.210
before steel-framed structures vaulted mankind upward.

00:00:09.210 --> 00:00:11.370
Cities exist to bring people together.

00:00:11.370 --> 00:00:14.400
And they have always been shaped by the transportation and construction

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.710
technologies of their eras.

00:00:16.710 --> 00:00:18.990
The pre-modern city was built for pedestrians

00:00:18.990 --> 00:00:22.020
who walked through often filthy streets and climbed

00:00:22.020 --> 00:00:24.690
dark stairwells to fifth floor garrets.

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:27.020
We have individual structures like the Colosseum

00:00:27.020 --> 00:00:29.790
and the Tower of London that survived from ancient cities.

00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:32.850
But we have fewer examples of whole cityscapes

00:00:32.850 --> 00:00:35.220
that remain as legacies of the past.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:39.780
Pompeii is one such example, frozen in time by the eruption of Vesuvius

00:00:39.780 --> 00:00:41.040
in 79 AD.

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:43.800
It provides us with a remarkable glimpse of urban life

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:46.320
in the age of Imperial Rome.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.200
The city had once been an independent ally of Rome.

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.810
But it became permanently part of the Roman world

00:00:51.810 --> 00:00:54.750
after it was occupied by Lucius Cornelius Sulla.

00:00:54.750 --> 00:00:58.800
The Romans built infrastructure, such as spectacular aqueducts, the multitude

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.820
of fountains that dark Pompeii testified to the importance of imported

00:01:02.820 --> 00:01:05.069
water for daily urban life.

00:01:05.069 --> 00:01:08.250
The local economy was, of course, largely agricultural.

00:01:08.250 --> 00:01:12.360
The rich volcanic soil nearby produced abundant grain and good wine.

00:01:12.360 --> 00:01:14.940
But it also had something of a tourist economy,

00:01:14.940 --> 00:01:17.550
as wealthy Romans built villas in Pompeii.

00:01:17.550 --> 00:01:21.750
The city is highly compact and well organized with a simple grid organizing

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:23.100
part of the town.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:24.900
The structures are typically short.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:28.200
Houses usually have one or two stories, although sometimes they

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:30.660
sit atop ruined older structures.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:34.260
Homes will often have an interior atrium, a typical Roman feature,

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:37.710
because the Mediterranean climate makes outdoor living ideal.

00:01:37.710 --> 00:01:40.710
Archaeologists have also identified about 100 benches

00:01:40.710 --> 00:01:44.040
outside the structures, which make it possible to comfortably wait

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:47.550
for entrance into the home of a powerful patron.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:50.040
Rooms are typically quite dark.

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:52.770
Glass windows seem to have been used by Romans only

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:54.660
after Pompeii's destruction.

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.830
Large openings would have exposed homes both to the elements and to thievery.

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:01.200
Exquisite paintings, both inside and out,

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:04.530
bring beauty into these otherwise dark structures.

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:08.190
Romans also saw little value in segregating work and home life.

00:02:08.190 --> 00:02:12.060
Workshops were often inserted into the ground floor of dwellings.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:15.450
Craftsman would sleep above and enjoy the shortest possible commutes

00:02:15.450 --> 00:02:16.860
down a flight of stairs.

00:02:16.860 --> 00:02:19.830
Bruges is a slightly more modern preserved city.

00:02:19.830 --> 00:02:22.800
It became frozen in time not by a volcano,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:26.580
but because the silting of the Zwin River in the late 15th century

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:29.430
radically reduced its economic importance.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:33.300
Consequently, this medieval megacity became Bruges-la-Morte,

00:02:33.300 --> 00:02:35.940
a dead town revealing its past.

00:02:35.940 --> 00:02:40.800
Whereas Pompeii had perhaps 11,000 inhabitants at its height, by 1400,

00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:43.840
Bruges had a population well over 100,000.

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:46.794
Bruges' vast wealth was built on trade in cloth

00:02:46.794 --> 00:02:49.710
which had originally been woven from the hairs of the shaggy sheep who

00:02:49.710 --> 00:02:51.180
live nearby.

00:02:51.180 --> 00:02:54.180
Like Venice, Bruges is a city of canals.

00:02:54.180 --> 00:02:58.290
In medieval times, it was easier to move goods on water than on land.

00:02:58.290 --> 00:03:02.580
Old Bruges is compact like Pompeii, built for walking and barges.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.120
Homes are taller, often reaching three or four stories,

00:03:06.120 --> 00:03:10.160
reflecting abundant demand for the limited space within the city's walls.

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:13.830
Bruges homes like atria but compensate with glass windows.

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:17.250
This colder city required more protection from the elements,

00:03:17.250 --> 00:03:18.480
including snow.

00:03:18.480 --> 00:03:22.440
And its distinct gables are both architectural adornment

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:26.190
and a sensible way to avoid the accumulation of snowfall.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:31.170
As the city grew richer, its wealthier inhabitants built up and out.

00:03:31.170 --> 00:03:33.990
The Gruuthuse, which housed aristocrats, is opulent,

00:03:33.990 --> 00:03:36.210
with four visible rows of windows.

00:03:36.210 --> 00:03:39.360
The house of the Buerse family also rises to four stories.

00:03:39.360 --> 00:03:43.200
That family began as innkeepers whose centrally located structure

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:47.580
became a meeting place for financiers, trading both knowledge and investments.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.910
Their name, which became Borse, now labels stock exchanges

00:03:50.910 --> 00:03:52.440
throughout the world.

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:56.070
Four or five stories was typically the upper limit on pre-elevator housing,

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:58.410
for who would want to walk up any higher?

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:02.130
The nicest floor, the piano nobile, was typically the second story,

00:04:02.130 --> 00:04:04.740
enjoying some protection from sloppy street life,

00:04:04.740 --> 00:04:06.540
but not requiring much climbing.

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:08.550
Upper floors were cheaper space providing

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:12.140
small garrets for the urban poor, such as the artistic protagonists of La

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:13.140
Boheme.

00:04:13.140 --> 00:04:15.450
Bruges has an abundance of public spaces,

00:04:15.450 --> 00:04:18.930
such as its striking market square which is loomed over

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:23.070
by a 270-foot belfry whose bells would toll out the time in an age

00:04:23.070 --> 00:04:24.270
before watches.

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:27.090
The tower sits atop the massive Cloth Hall,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.790
which is something of a cathedral to trade in wool.

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.920
But while the merchants of Bruges pointed forward

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:34.890
towards our more secular age, the city's architecture

00:04:34.890 --> 00:04:39.450
reminds us that even in that commercial city, religion ranked first.

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:42.090
The tallest structure in Bruges is the 400-foot tower

00:04:42.090 --> 00:04:43.980
of the Church of Our Lady.

00:04:43.980 --> 00:04:47.970
Indeed, church towers were the dominant feature of most medieval skylines.

00:04:47.970 --> 00:04:50.550
And many of the great feats of medieval architecture,

00:04:50.550 --> 00:04:54.210
such as flying buttresses, were aimed at allowing Gothic churches to inspire

00:04:54.210 --> 00:04:56.580
religious feeling through sacred space.

00:04:56.580 --> 00:05:00.310
Command of glass brought light and beauty inside.

00:05:00.310 --> 00:05:04.030
Urban Europe is filled with pre-modern structures that teach us history.

00:05:04.030 --> 00:05:06.010
The massive Palazzi of Florence that belonged

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:08.050
to families such as the Medici and the Strozzi

00:05:08.050 --> 00:05:11.440
remind us both of their mercantile prosperity and the dangers

00:05:11.440 --> 00:05:13.790
of life in 15th century Florence.

00:05:13.790 --> 00:05:17.020
They are built like fortresses, which is perhaps unsurprising, given

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:20.020
the propensity of medieval Florentines to break out into violence,

00:05:20.020 --> 00:05:22.180
such as the Revolt of the Ciompi.

00:05:22.180 --> 00:05:24.520
Bruges' buildings are far less secure, suggesting

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:27.190
that they were built in a safer city.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.430
Paris' Place de Vosges, with its elegant four-story uniformity,

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.380
reminds us of the efforts of French rulers

00:05:32.380 --> 00:05:35.190
to impose order onto urban chaos.

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:38.620
The curving Georgian elegance of Regent Street in London

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:41.950
or the Royal Crescent in Bath reminds us that uniformity can also

00:05:41.950 --> 00:05:46.420
come from private developers attempting to sell aesthetic grandeur.

00:05:46.420 --> 00:05:49.000
These structures reflect the wealth of industrializing

00:05:49.000 --> 00:05:52.480
England that was poured into growing consumer cities.

00:05:52.480 --> 00:05:55.570
As London grew richer and larger, the demand for space

00:05:55.570 --> 00:05:57.760
propelled buildings upward.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:06:03.580
The Midland Grand Hotel, built at St. Pancras Station, soars to 270 feet.

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:05.540
Elevators made height easier.

00:06:05.540 --> 00:06:09.550
But the Midland Grand still has a spectacular central staircase.

00:06:09.550 --> 00:06:11.800
The structure's vast medieval walls remind us

00:06:11.800 --> 00:06:15.220
of the difficulty of building up before steel frames.

00:06:15.220 --> 00:06:18.340
Today, we are charmed by pre-modern urban spaces.

00:06:18.340 --> 00:06:21.760
They are so fundamentally different than our elevator-oriented skyscrapers

00:06:21.760 --> 00:06:23.680
and car-based suburbs.

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:28.240
Yet, these pre-modern spaces were still about enabling mankind to work together

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:30.430
in urban agglomerations.

00:06:30.430 --> 00:06:33.310
Older urban spaces provide a window into the lives

00:06:33.310 --> 00:06:38.760
of the past that are both so similar and so different from our lives today.

